+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Reviewer-authored command logger. Usage:
+#   run_logged.sh LOGFILE COMMAND [ARG ...]
+set +e
+
+log_file=$1
+shift
+
+{
+  printf 'CWD: %q\n' "$PWD"
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+  printf '%s\n' '--- OUTPUT BEGIN ---'
+  "$@"
+  command_status=$?
+  printf '%s\n' '--- OUTPUT END ---'
+  printf 'EXIT STATUS: %d\n' "$command_status"
+  exit "$command_status"
+} >"$log_file" 2>&1

codex
The LLVM smoke run exposed a generated-semantics portability defect: its `[simplification]`-only rational helper equations leave `negRat(rat(1,1))` residual under the LLVM interpreter, so all LLVM executions exit 113. This is a candidate semantics issue, not an audit infrastructure failure. I’m preserving it and rebuilding the standalone generated semantics with the Haskell backend—the backend the candidate actually targeted—to complete the required concrete comparison.
